'''weave<sup>1</sup>''' (wēv), ''v.''; pret. ''wove'' (formerly also ''weaved''), pp. ''woven'' (sometimes ''wove'' and formerly also ''weaved''), ppr. ''weaving''. [< ME. ''weven'' (pret. ''waf'', ''wof'', pl. ''weven'', ''woven'', pp. ''woven''), < AS. ''wefan'' (pret. ''wæf'', pp. ''wefen'') = MD. D. ''weven'' = OHG. ''weban'', MHG. G. ''weben'' = Icel. ''vefa'' = Sw. ''väfva'' = Dan. ''væve'', weave (connection with Goth. ''bi-waibjan'', wrap around, is doubtful), = Gr. √ ὑφ (orig. √ Ϝαφ), in ὑφή, ὕφος, a web, ὑφαίνειν, weave; cf. Skt. ''ūrna-vābhi'', a spider, lit. ‘wool-weaver,’ Skt. √ ''va'', weave, also Lith. ''woras'', a spinner, spider. From the root of ''weave''<sup>1</sup> are ult. E. ''web'', ''weft''<sup>1</sup>, ''woof'', ''oof'', ''abb'', etc.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To form by interlacing flexible parts, such as threads, yarns, filaments, or strips of different materials. See ''weaving''.

{{smaller block|Where the women ''wove'' hangings for the grove.
{{right|2 Ki. xxiii. 7.}}
And now his ''woven'' girths he breaks asunder.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 266.}}
<poem>To wanton Dalliance negligently laid,
We ''weave'' the Chaplet, and we crown the Bowl.</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Solomon, ii.}}
These purple vests were ''weaved'' by Dardan dames.
{{right|''Dryden.''}}}}

'''2.''' To form a texture from; interlace or entwine into a fabric.

{{smaller block|When she ''weaved'' the sleided silk.
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, iv., Prol., l. 21.}}}}

'''3.''' To entwine; unite by intermixture or close connection; insert by or as by weaving.

{{smaller block|She ''waf'' it wel, and wroot the story above.}}

