'''weaver''' (wē′vėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wevere'', ''wevar'', < AS *''wefere'' = MD. D. ''wever'' = OHG. ''weberi'', MHG. ''webære'', G. ''weber'' = Sw. ''väfvare'' = Dan. ''væver'', a weaver; as ''weave''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''webber.''] '''1.''' One who weaves; one whose occupation is weaving.
{{smaller block|''Weivars'' also of wolne and lynnyn.
{{right|Quoted in ''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), Pref., p. xlvii.}}
''Weavers'' were supposed to be generally good singers. Their trade being sedentary, they had an opportunity of practising, and sometimes in parts, while they were at work. Warburton adds that many of the ''weavers'' in Queen Elizabeth’s days were Flemish Calvinists, who fled from the persecution of the Duke of Alva, and were therefore particularly given to singing psalms. . . . Hence the exclamation of Falstaff, “I would I were a ''weaver!'' I could sing psalms, and all manner of songs.” {{right|''Nares.''}}}}
'''2.''' In ''ornith.'', a weaver-bird.—'''3.''' In ''entom.'': (''a'') A gyrinid beetle; a whirligig: so called from its intricate circlings and gyrations on the surface of the water. See ''whirligig'', 4, and cut under ''Gyrinidæ.'' (''b'') A spinning-spider; a true araneid which weaves a web. {{smaller|Various groups of such spiders are distinguished by the form of their webs, as ''line-weavers'', ''orb-weavers'', ''tapestry-weavers'', ''tube-weavers'', ''tunnel-weavers'', etc. See ''spider.''}} '''4.''' In ''ichth.'', same as ''weever.''—{{smaller|'''Mahali weaver.''' See ''weaver-bird.''—'''Sociable weaver.''' See ''weaver-bird.''—'''Tapestry weaver.''' See ''tapestry.''—'''Weavers’ bottom''', a chronic inflammation of a bursa situated over the tuberosity of the ischium, occurring as a result of sitting long and constantly on a hard seat.—'''Yellow-crowned weaver.''' See ''weaver-bird.''}}

'''weaver-bird''' (wē′vėr-bėrd), ''n.'' One of numerous Old World (chiefly African and Indian) conirostral passerine birds, noted for the dex-

